        "title": "From Zero to Live: Setting Up My Jekyll Blog with GitHub Pages and AWS Route 53",
        "excerpt":"   Note: The AWS Route53 domain must be fully configured before using this method. Optionally one can use github pages address to render the blog/website.    In less than a week I went from zero to a fully deployed, professional blog at datainsidedata.com.   This wasn’t just a “hello world” experiment. I wanted a lean, pro-grade stack for this project using:   ✔ Jekyll (static site generator)  ✔ GitHub Pages (free hosting)  ✔ AWS Route 53 (custom domain + DNS)      This post provides a walkthrough of how I did it, what I learned, and how you can replicate (or improve) the setup for your business, nonprofit, or personal brand.   I provide a deep dive into building a blog using Minima theme.   The Stack                  Tool       Purpose                       Jekyll       Static site generator                 GitHub Pages       Free hosting for site                 AWS Route 53       DNS + custom domain                 VS Code       Local development                 datainsidedata.com       My custom domain           💡 Why Jekyll Instead of Just HTML/CSS/JS?   When I started this, I asked myself: “Why not just code a static site by hand?”   Here’s why I chose Jekyll:   Built for Blogs   Jekyll was literally designed for blogs. It supports:      Markdown posts   Categories &amp; tags out of the box   Auto-generated archive &amp; pagination   Clean permalinks like /2026/07/15/my-jekyll.html   With pure HTML/CSS/JS, I’d have to hand-code all of that.   Minimalist and Professional   Jekyll doesn’t need a heavy backend (no PHP, no databases).  It’s just static files—fast, secure, and deploys beautifully on GitHub Pages.   Gems and Themes = Less Reinventing the Wheel      The Minimal Mistakes theme gave me a professional design instantly.   Gems like jekyll-feed and jekyll-seo-tag handle RSS feeds &amp; SEO for me.   Pure HTML/CSS/JS? I’d be reinventing:  ✔ Sitemap.xml  ✔ RSS feed  ✔ SEO metadata  ✔ Responsive layouts   One Command → Whole Site Built   Jekyll lets me run:   bundle exec jekyll build   …and it renders all posts, layouts, and assets into the _site folder, ready for deployment.   In a pure static setup, I’d manually copy/paste and keep track of every link. 😬   📦 Initial Setup: Jekyll + Minimal Mistakes Theme   Install Ruby, Bundler, and Jekyll   gem install bundler jekyll   Create a new Jekyll site   jekyll new datainsidedata-website cd datainsidedata-website bundle install   Add the Minimal Mistakes theme for a clean, professional look   Configure _config.yml      Set site title, description, author info   Enable pagination &amp; pretty permalinks   Set up categories &amp; tags   📂 Project Structure (before build):   _data/ _includes/ _layouts/ _pages/ _posts/ _site/  (auto-generated by Jekyll build)   🌐 Custom Domain with GitHub Pages + AWS Route 53   Private + Public Repo Workflow   Since GitHub Pages doesn’t serve from private repos unless you pay for Pro:      Private repo: did-site-private (holds full Jekyll source)   Public repo: did-site-public (hosts built site for Pages)   👨🏾‍💻 Local workflow:   # Build site bundle exec jekyll build  # Push to public repo cd _site git init git remote add origin https://github.com/dataeden/did-site-public.git git add . git commit -m \"Deploy\" git push -u origin main   AWS Route 53 DNS Setup   Add A records:   185.199.108.153 185.199.109.153 185.199.110.153 185.199.111.153   Add a CNAME file in the public repo:   datainsidedata.com   Verify domain with GitHub:  Add a TXT record in Route 53 like:   _github-pages-challenge.datainsidedata.com  TXT  \"abc123xyz456\"   Enable HTTPS in GitHub Pages after TLS cert provision.   Sneak Peek: Automating with GitHub Actions (No More _site Pushes!)   Here’s the workflow I’m planning:   📂 .github/workflows/deploy.yml   name: Build and Deploy Jekyll Site  on:   push:     branches:       - main  # Watch for changes in private repo  jobs:   build-deploy:     runs-on: ubuntu-latest      steps:     - name: Checkout private repo       uses: actions/checkout@v3      - name: Setup Ruby + Jekyll       uses: ruby/setup-ruby@v1       with:         ruby-version: '3.1'      - name: Install dependencies       run: bundle install      - name: Build site       run: bundle exec jekyll build      - name: Deploy to public repo       uses: peaceiris/actions-gh-pages@v3       with:         github_token: $         publish_dir: ./_site         publish_branch: main         destination_repo: dataeden/did-site-public   With this in place:      Push to did-site-private   GitHub Actions auto-builds &amp; deploys to did-site-public   No more manual _site pushes 🚀   Lessons Learned   💥 _pages folder pain: Jekyll didn’t respect links until I restructured pages.  🔥 Custom nav bar: Built my own in bash _includes/header.html + _includes/nav.html.  🕒 DNS propagation: Patience and coffee are dev tools too.   The Result   ✅ Live at datainsidedata.com  ✅ Clean navigation, custom logo, HTTPS-secured  ✅ Learned Jekyll + DNS at a pro level   What’s Next?   ✔ Publish more posts (like this one!)  ✔ Add Google Analytics  ✔ Fully automate deploys with GitHub Actions  ✔ Share more dev journey content   📺 Watch the full video walkthrough → [Coming soon!]     Data Inside Data™.   Tech Hands, a science Mind, and a Heart for Community™.  ","categories": ["github-pages","aws-route53","jekyll"],
        "tags": ["blog","devsecops","static sites","aws","ruby","cli","html","css","markdown"],
        "url": "/did-builder-showcase-staging/blog/2026/01/02/from-zero-to-live-jekyll-blog/",
        "teaser": "/did-builder-showcase-staging/assets/images/thumbs/site-wide-archive-post-thumb.png"
